@** Shared parameters. The CPU and the GPU must agree on one thing above all:
the layout of the data that the host hands to every OptiX launch. That data is
described once, in \lpfile{LaunchParams.h}, and the header is compiled twice:
by the host C++ compiler for \lpfile{OptixRenderer.cpp}, which fills the
structures in, and by \texttt{nvcc} for \lpfile{devicePrograms.cu}, which reads
them. Because both sides see the very same declarations, a field added here
cannot be forgotten on one side; it can only be left unset.

<<UsdOptixViewer/src/LaunchParams.h>>=
<<Opening of the shared parameters header>>

<<The background mesh id and the ray types>>

<<The camera frame>>

<<Scalar material inputs>>

<<Material data on the device>>

<<Mesh data on the device>>

<<The launch parameters>>
@ The header includes only CUDA and OptiX type definitions, never their
function declarations. From OptiX we take [[OptixTraversableHandle]], from
CUDA's texture types [[cudaTextureObject_t]], and from CUDA's vector types
[[float3]], [[float4]], [[uchar4]] and friends. These headers declare the
same sizes and the same alignments for the host compiler and for
\texttt{nvcc}, which is what makes a byte-for-byte copy from one side to the
other legitimate.

<<Opening of the shared parameters header>>=
// Data shared between the host (OptixRenderer.cpp) and the OptiX programs
// (devicePrograms.cu).
#pragma once

#include <optix_types.h>
#include <texture_types.h>
#include <vector_types.h>
@ When a camera ray hits nothing, the pixel belongs to no mesh. We mark such
pixels with the largest unsigned integer, [[kNoMesh]], which can never be a
valid mesh index. The application reads these ids back to implement picking:
a click on the background clears the selection.

The renderer traces two kinds of rays. \emph{Radiance} rays ask ``what colour
arrives along this ray?''; \emph{shadow} rays only ask ``is the light visible
from here?''. OptiX selects the program that handles a miss, and the record
that handles a hit, by an index into the shader binding table; the enumeration
gives these indices names. [[RAY_TYPE_COUNT]] is the number of hit records,
one per ray type, and the stride that every call of [[optixTrace]] passes for
them.

<<The background mesh id and the ray types>>=
constexpr unsigned int kNoMesh = 0xffffffffu;

enum RayType { RAY_TYPE_RADIANCE = 0, RAY_TYPE_SHADOW = 1, RAY_TYPE_COUNT };
@ The camera is usually a pinhole. Rather than storing a field of view and an
aspect ratio, the host precomputes three vectors: [[w]] is the unit vector
from the eye towards the centre of the image plane, while [[u]] and [[v]]
point from that centre to the middle of the right and top edges of the image
when the plane is at distance 1. A point of the image with normalized device
coordinates $(x,y)\in[-1,1]^2$ then lies in the direction $w + x\,u + y\,v$,
and the ray generation program needs no trigonometry at all. If $\phi$ is the
vertical field of view and $a$ the aspect ratio, the host sets $|w|=1$,
$|v|=\tan(\phi/2)$ and $|u|=a\tan(\phi/2)$.

A USD camera may also be orthographic. Then all rays are parallel to [[w]] and
the image point moves the origin instead: the ray leaves $e + x\,u + y\,v$,
and $|u|$, $|v|$ are half the width and height of the view in scene units.
USD cameras also carry a clipping range. The renderer honours it for camera
rays, as a depth measured along [[w]], so that a near plane can cut away the
wall in front of an interior shot. The orbit camera uses $[0, 10^{16}]$.

The eyes of a headset have asymmetric frusta: the image plane is not centred
on the viewing direction. Such a camera still fits the formula if [[w]] points
at the centre of the window rather than straight ahead; it is then no longer
a unit vector, which matters only to the clipping range, so XR eyes use none.

<<The camera frame>>=
// Perspective: rays leave `eye` through w + u * x + v * y, for x, y in [-1, 1]
// (u, v span the image plane at distance 1). Orthographic: rays leave
// eye + u * x + v * y along w (u, v are half the view size in scene units).
// Camera rays only see hits between clipNear and clipFar along w.
struct Camera
{
    float3 eye;
    float3 u, v, w;  // right, up and forward (w normalized, except for XR eyes, which have no clipping)
    unsigned int orthographic;
    float clipNear, clipFar;
};
@ Many inputs of a UsdPreviewSurface material, such as roughness or metallic,
are single numbers that may either be constant or come from one channel of a
texture. A [[ScalarParam]] holds both possibilities. When [[texture]] is
negative the value is [[value]]; otherwise it is channel [[channel]] (0 to 3
for red, green, blue and alpha) of the texture, mapped through the
[[scale]] and [[bias]] that the UsdUVTexture node specifies for that channel.
Keeping the channel number lets one texture carry several inputs, as in the
common ``packed'' maps where green is roughness and blue is metallic.

<<Scalar material inputs>>=
// A float input: `value`, or texture[channel] * scale + bias when texture >= 0.
struct ScalarParam
{
    float value;
    int   texture;
    int   channel;
    float scale, bias;
};
@ Materials. The scene part of the program reduces every UsdPreviewSurface it
meets to one [[MaterialData]], and each triangle refers to one of them by
index. The comment summarizes how the closest-hit program interprets the
fields; we shall see each formula again when it is used.

<<Material data on the device>>=
// UsdPreviewSurface subset.
//   diffuse  = texture(st) * texScale + texBias, or `color` when untextured
//   cutout   = hits where opacityTexture(st)[opacityChannel] < opacityThreshold are ignored
//   normal   = normalTexture(st) * normalScale + normalBias (tangent space)
//   specular = GGX; F0 from metallic + ior, or specColor in the specular workflow
struct MaterialData
{
    <<Texture remapping coefficients of a material>>
    <<Colors, textures and inputs of a material>>
};
@ The six vectors are the [[scale]] and [[bias]] inputs of the UsdUVTexture
nodes that feed the diffuse color, the normal and the specular color. They
are applied componentwise to every texel, $c = s\cdot t + b$. For a normal map
the specification asks for $s=(2,2,2)$ and $b=(-1,-1,-1)$, which maps the
stored range $[0,1]$ to the vector range $[-1,1]$; files that use the
DirectX convention flip the green channel through these same coefficients.

These vectors open the structure for a reason of layout. A [[float4]] must be
aligned on a 16-byte boundary, while a [[float3]], an [[int]] or a [[float]]
needs only 4 bytes. Placed at the head of the structure, which is itself
16-byte aligned, the six [[float4]] members fill its first 96 bytes with no
gap between them.

<<Texture remapping coefficients of a material>>=
float4 texScale;
float4 texBias;
float4 normalScale;
float4 normalBias;
float4 specScale;
float4 specBias;
@ Then come the remaining inputs. [[color]] is the diffuse color of an
untextured material. [[specColor]] and [[specTexture]] are used only in the
\emph{specular workflow}, where [[specularWorkflow]] is nonzero and the
reflectance at normal incidence is given directly; otherwise the
\emph{metallic workflow} derives it from [[metallic]] and the index of
refraction [[ior]]. The opacity fields describe an alpha cutout: the
[[opacityChannel]] of [[opacityTexture]] is compared with
[[opacityThreshold]].

Every one of these members needs only 4-byte alignment, so none of them
forces the compiler to insert padding. Each [[float3]] is followed by an
[[int]], which completes a 16-byte line, and the two [[ScalarParam]] records
of 20 bytes each are followed by six 4-byte fields, which bring the total to
192 bytes, a multiple of 16. No padding is needed at the end either, and the
structure has the same size and the same layout on both sides of the bus.
Texture references are indices into the array of CUDA texture objects of
<<The scene as seen by the device>>, with $-1$ meaning ``no texture''.

<<Colors, textures and inputs of a material>>=
float3 color;
int    texture;  // index into LaunchParams::textures, -1 for none
float3 specColor;
int    specTexture;
ScalarParam roughness;
ScalarParam metallic;
int    opacityTexture;
int    opacityChannel;
float  opacityThreshold;
int    normalTexture;
int    specularWorkflow;
float  ior;
@* Meshes. The scene keeps every renderable prim as one indexed triangle mesh
in the prim's own local space, and the renderer builds a geometry
acceleration structure (GAS) for each of them. One instance acceleration
structure (IAS) then places the meshes in the world: instance~$i$ carries the
transform of mesh~$i$ and points to its GAS. Since the instance id that the
renderer gives to instance~$i$ is $i$ itself, the closest-hit program learns
from [[optixGetInstanceId]] both which [[MeshData]] to read and which mesh id
to report for picking.

A [[MeshData]] holds device pointers to the arrays of one mesh. Vertices are
indexed: [[indices]] gives the three vertices of each triangle, and
[[positions]], [[normals]] and [[uvs]] are per vertex, so a vertex has one
position, one shading normal and one pair of texture coordinates. Face-varying
data, where the corner of one face needs a value different from the same
point on the neighbouring face, is handled by the scene before it gets here:
such corners simply become different vertices. A mesh without smooth normals
has a null [[normals]] pointer and is shaded with the flat geometric normal;
one without texture coordinates has a null [[uvs]]. [[triMaterial]] gives the
material of each triangle, since USD geometry subsets may bind different
materials to the faces of one mesh. All of these are in local space; the
device programs carry hit points and normals to world space with the
transforms of the instance.

<<Mesh data on the device>>=
// One mesh, in its local space. Instance i of the IAS is mesh i, so
// optixGetInstanceId() is both the index into LaunchParams::meshes and the
// mesh id used for picking.
struct MeshData
{
    const float3*       positions;
    const float3*       normals;      // shading normal per vertex; null: flat
    const float2*       uvs;          // st per vertex; null: no texture coordinates
    const uint3*        indices;      // 3 vertices per triangle
    const unsigned int* triMaterial;  // material of each triangle
};
@* The launch parameters. Everything a launch needs is gathered in a single
structure. The host copies it to a small device buffer before each launch,
and OptiX makes it visible to all programs as the [[__constant__]] variable
[[params]]. Constant memory is cached and broadcast to all threads of a warp,
which suits data that every ray reads and no ray writes; the output buffers it
points to, of course, live in ordinary global memory.

<<The launch parameters>>=
struct LaunchParams
{
    <<Output buffers of a launch>>

    <<Camera, light and rendering switches>>

    <<The scene as seen by the device>>
};
@ The output of a launch. Rendering is progressive: each launch traces one new
sample per pixel. [[accumBuffer]] keeps, for every pixel, the running sum of
the samples in its first three components and their number in the fourth, and
[[colorBuffer]] receives their average, encoded for display. [[frameIndex]]
counts the samples already taken; the host sets it back to zero whenever the
camera, the scene or any option changes, and the next launch then overwrites
the sums instead of adding to them. [[meshIdBuffer]] records which mesh each
pixel shows, and [[hitBuffer]] the world-space point it shows, with a fourth
component of~1 (0 for the background); the orbit camera turns around such
points. Both are written only on frame~0, whose rays go through the pixel
centres. [[hitBuffer]] may be null, for views that need no points.

<<Output buffers of a launch>>=
// output: colorBuffer = accumBuffer / (frameIndex + 1); meshIdBuffer and hitBuffer are written on frame 0
uchar4*       colorBuffer;
float4*       accumBuffer;
unsigned int* meshIdBuffer;  // mesh under each pixel (kNoMesh for background)
float4*       hitBuffer;     // world-space point under each pixel, w = 1 (w = 0 for background); may be null
unsigned int  width, height;
unsigned int  frameIndex;    // progressive sample index, reset to 0 after any change
@ Next come the camera, the single directional light, and the switches of
the \emph{View} menu, each an integer used as a boolean. Turning a switch off
does not change the scene, only how it is drawn, which is handy for comparing
the effect of a feature. [[selectedMesh]] is the mesh highlighted in orange,
or [[kNoMesh]].

<<Camera, light and rendering switches>>=
Camera camera;
float3 lightDir;  // direction towards the light, normalized
unsigned int shadows;
unsigned int mipmaps;      // 0: always sample the finest texture level
unsigned int normalMaps;   // 0: ignore normal maps
unsigned int reflections;  // 0: reflect the sky only, without tracing rays
unsigned int selectedMesh;
@ The scene. [[handle]] is the traversable handle of the IAS, the one that
[[optixTrace]] needs. [[meshes]] is the array of [[MeshData]] records,
indexed by instance id, [[materials]] the array of materials that
[[triMaterial]] refers to, and [[textures]] the CUDA texture objects that
the materials refer to by index.

<<The scene as seen by the device>>=
// scene: an IAS with one instance (and one GAS) per mesh
OptixTraversableHandle handle;
const MeshData*     meshes;
const MaterialData* materials;
const cudaTextureObject_t* textures;
